+    "A QCQP is still nonconvex because the pose constraints are nonconvex. To form the semidefinite relaxation, we lift $x$ to the moment matrix $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & x^{\\mathsf T}\\\\ x & X\\end{bmatrix}$ with $X=xx^{\\mathsf T}$. The quadratic objective and constraints become linear in $x$ and $X$; dropping the nonconvex condition $\\operatorname{rank}(X)=1$ produces a convex semidefinite program whose optimum is a lower bound on the original minimization problem. If the optimal lifted blocks are rank one, the relaxation is tight and their dominant eigenvectors recover a globally optimal feasible QCQP solution. Rank one is sufficient but not necessary for tightness: even when an SDP solution has higher rank, a feasible local solution is certified globally optimal whenever its objective matches the SDP lower bound, up to numerical tolerance.\n",
     "\n",
